WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.250
In this portion of coverage of random methods,
we will look at randomizing as it relates

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.520
to words and phrases.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:13.380
The first such method is the shuffle method,
which rearranges the order of a list.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:21.420
In this 623-shuffle file, I am using the shuffle
method, shown on line 3, to arrange categories

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:27.470
for a quiz on the periodic table of the elements,
related to chemistry, which are stored in

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:32.149
a list named categories on line 2.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:36.420
Notice the import on line 1 looks different
than the previous import statements on the

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.220
random module library.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.059
Rather than import one element of the library,
we are importing the entire library so we

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:47.410
can use any module within the library.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:53.859
I will run this code and we see the categories
in a different order, which would then dictate

00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:58.719
the order of categories being asked about
for a chemistry quiz.

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:03.630
From shuffle, we move to the choice module
within the random library.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:10.189
Choice, as you may have guessed from the name
of the module, chooses a value, at random,

00:01:10.189 --> 00:01:12.320
from a list of values.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:20.510
In this 623-choice file, we have the same
import statement on line 1 and the same categories

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.939
list on line 2.

00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:30.510
However, the code on line 3 is different,
as the shuffle method has been replaced with

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:34.720
the choice method on the categories list.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.369
Because we are extracting one value from the
list of categories, we are storing that choice

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:42.170
in a variable-named category.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:47.969
Then, in line 5, we see a print statement
to print the random category chosen from the

00:01:47.969 --> 00:01:49.329
categories list.

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:58.450
I will run this code, and indeed, each time
I run the code, we see one category randomly

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:02.600
chosen from the list of categories.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:08.129
While a choice returns one random value from
a list, the sample method returns multiple

00:02:08.129 --> 00:02:10.610
random values from a list.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:16.860
Suppose we want to get two questions from
two categories within this list.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:21.080
To return multiple random values from a list,
we will want to use the sample method instead

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.230
of the choice method.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:31.150
On line 3, I will change the category variable
to random.sample, and then, in parentheses,

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:37.280
we need two arguments: the list and the number
of unique values to get from the list.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:46.090
Thus, I will enter categories and then 2 for
the two needed arguments.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:53.420
I will then run the code, and we get two categories
returned as a list.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.980
One more point to make about sample versus
choice.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.360
You could run the choice method twice here,
but there is no guarantee you will get two

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.730
unique values returned.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:08.610
If you want, for example, to ensure a question
comes out of two different categories before

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:12.920
a category is repeated, use the sample method.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:17.940
This topic concludes the domain and all the
exam topics in this course.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.480
Like we did at the halfway point in this course,
